So the mutation removes the first `__syncthreads()` after writing to shared memory, but keeps the one inside the loop. This is a critical synchronization point - all threads need to finish writing their values to shared memory before any thread reads from it. Without that initial barrier, threads could read stale or uninitialized values during the reduction loop, causing incorrect results or race conditions. Looking at the original and mutated code side by side, the mutation removes the `__syncthreads()` call that appears after `shared[tid] = val;` and before the loop starts. The synchronization inside the loop remains intact. This initial barrier ensures all threads have finished writing to shared memory before any thread begins reading during the reduction phase.

Without that first synchronization, threads performing the reduction could read stale or uninitialized values. For instance, when stride equals 128 in a 256-thread block, threads 0-127 attempt to read from indices 128-255 in shared memory. If threads 128-255 haven't completed their writes yet, the reads return garbage data, corrupting the reduction result.

